; pi4Atomics.pi4 - the atomics, on the silicon. Read the feature
;                   register that decides whether the ARMv8.1 LSE
;                   instructions exist, then exercise the ARMv8.0
;                   exclusives and RaspberryPi4/Lib/atomic.pi4 and
;                   report what the part actually did.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   PureMetalForge.exe --compile RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/NotBuilding/pi4Atomics.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o atomics.img
;
;   pmf> deadman 15
;   pmf> b 400000 <len> <crc>
;   pmf> run 400000
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS EXISTS - TWO QUESTIONS NO GATE ON THIS MACHINE CAN ANSWER
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; QUESTION ONE: DOES THIS PART HAVE THE LSE ATOMICS?
;
; A64Assembler.pbi REFUSES cas, swp, ldadd and the rest of the ARMv8.1
; Large System Extension family, and its refusal message names
; ID_AA64ISAR0_EL1.ATOMIC as the thing to check. The reasoning behind
; that refusal is that the Cortex-A72 is an ARMv8.0-A core and the
; extension arrived in ARMv8.1, so the instructions would be UNDEFINED
; on the part - the manual's decode pseudocode for every one of them
; reads `if !IsFeatureImplemented(FEAT_LSE) then EndOfDecode(Decode_UNDEF)`
; at Datasheets/arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:6169 and its siblings.
;
; That reasoning is sound and it is still REASONING. This program reads
; the register.
;
; THE FIELD: ATOMIC, bits 23:20, with 0b0000 meaning not implemented,
; 0b0010 meaning FEAT_LSE and 0b0011 meaning FEAT_LSE128 -
; RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_arm64_tools_sysreg:1349-1353. Note there
; is no 0b0001. A test of this field against 1 is a silent always-false;
; the test is against 0.
;
; NO LSE INSTRUCTION IS EXECUTED HERE, and that is the point. If the
; field reads 0 then executing one would be an undefined-instruction
; exception into a vector table nothing in this tree installs - a hang
; with no output. The register is read instead.
;
; QUESTION TWO, AND IT IS THE ONE NOBODY HAS ASKED: DO THE EXCLUSIVES
; WORK AT ALL ON THIS BOARD, WITH THE MMU OFF?
;
; This is not paranoia and it is not covered by any encoding check.
; Every payload in this tree runs with the MMU off unless it says
; otherwise, and with the MMU off ARMv8-A treats every data access as
; Device memory. The architecture does not promise that Load/Store
; Exclusive works on Device memory - the global monitor is an
; implementation-defined structure and a part is permitted not to
; support exclusives outside Normal memory.
;
; THAT RULE IS NOT CITED HERE, deliberately, and the honesty matters:
; it lives in the memory-model chapter of Arm DDI 0487, which is NOT on
; this machine. Datasheets/arm-a64-instruction-set.txt is the
; instruction-set volume and carries encodings and per-instruction
; pseudocode only - a grep of it for the Device-memory rule finds
; nothing. So the paragraph above is reasoning from what the exclusive
; monitor IS, not a quotation, and this program exists to replace it
; with a measurement.
;
; THE STAKES: if a store-exclusive can never succeed on this board's
; memory as currently mapped, then every primitive in
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/atomic.pi4 returns #ATOMIC_GAVE_UP, always, and the
; bounded retry is the only reason that is a refusal rather than a hang.
; That would be a real finding and it would say that locks on this part
; need the MMU on with DRAM mapped Normal - which is
; RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4's job, not this file's.
;
; Check 3 below is the one that answers it, and it is the check to read
; first if anything else looks strange.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS IS SAFE, argued rather than asserted
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every instruction here is an mrs, an ordinary load or store, or an
; exclusive access to a word in this program's own BSS. Nothing is
; written to any system register. No peripheral is touched except the
; UART. There is no LSE instruction and no floating-point instruction,
; so there is nothing here that can be UNDEFINED on this part.
;
; The one construct that could hang is a retry loop, and every loop
; reached from this file is bounded by atomic.pi4's #ATOMIC_SPINS - the
; whole reason that bound exists. The raw checks below use no loop at
; all.
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN ANYWAY. `deadman 15` before `run`, every time, for the
; reason in the vault's "Bench practice - arm the deadman": the habit is
; the thing being maintained, and the one session that skipped it turned
; a recoverable hang into a request for the mains.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT EACH CHECK PROVES, AND WHAT NONE OF THEM CAN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; These run on ONE core. Nothing here contends anything, so nothing here
; proves a lock excludes anybody. What they prove is that the monitor
; behaves as the architecture describes on this silicon, and that the
; library's arithmetic and its refusals are right - which is everything
; except the thing that needs a second core.
;
; Two properties are deliberately NOT asserted, because on real silicon
; they are IMPLEMENTATION DEFINED and asserting them would be inventing
; a requirement:
;
;   * that a store-exclusive to a DIFFERENT address than the load fails.
;     AArch64_IsExclusiveVA is an optional check and the manual says
;     plainly "It is always safe to return TRUE"
;     (arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:481596-481604). This part is allowed
;     to succeed there. The value is PRINTED, not judged.
;
;   * that a plain store between the pair clears the reservation. It
;     does on every implementation anyone has met, and the architecture
;     leaves room. Printed, not judged.
;
; The interpreter's model IS strict about both, which is what makes it
; useful for catching a program that leans on the sloppy case. A model
; being stricter than the part is the safe direction; the reverse is how
; four gates in this project certified real bugs this week.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/atomic.pi4"

; The words under test. Each gets its own #ATOMIC_PAD_WORDS block so no
; two of them can share a reservation granule - which is the padding
; rule atomic.pi4's header states, applied to the program that is
; supposed to demonstrate it.
Global Dim at_lock.i[#ATOMIC_PAD_WORDS]
Global Dim at_counter.i[#ATOMIC_PAD_WORDS]
Global Dim at_scratch.i[#ATOMIC_PAD_WORDS]
Global Dim at_other.i[#ATOMIC_PAD_WORDS]
Global at_result.i
Global at_pass.i
Global at_fail.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The system-register read. One mrs, result left in x0, which is where
;  a .i return value goes - the idiom from
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/timer.pi4:221-223 and pi4FpState.pi4.
;
;  The encoding for id_aa64isar0_el1 was added to A64SystemRegBase() in
;  A64Assembler.pbi on 2026-08-28, cited to
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/llvm19.1.0_AArch64SystemOperands.td:752 with
;  the quintuple independently confirmed by
;  RaspberryPi4/Reference/v6.12_arm64_tools_sysreg:1306.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i AtIdIsar0()
  ASM
    mrs x0, id_aa64isar0_el1
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i AtMidr()
  ASM
    mrs x0, midr_el1
  EndASM
EndProcedure

Procedure.i AtCurrentEl()
  ASM
    mrs x0, currentel
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE RAW MONITOR PROBES.
;
;  These bypass atomic.pi4 on purpose. The library's loops would retry
;  a failure away, and a failure is exactly what three of these are
;  measuring. Each is a straight line of at most five instructions and
;  contains no branch backwards, so none of them can hang.
;
;  All of them take the address in x0 and leave a status in x0, using
;  x9..x11 as working registers - the same discipline atomic.pi4's
;  header sets out, for the same reason: a global read inside one of
;  these would make it a different test on a second core.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------

; A store-exclusive with NO reservation. clrex guarantees the monitor is
; open first, so this is a deterministic architectural requirement and
; not a probabilistic one: it MUST return 1.
Procedure.i AtStxrWithNoReservation(*addr)
  ASM
    clrex
    movz  x9, #$5A5A
    stxr  w10, x9, [x0]
    mov   x0, x10
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; A matched pair. Returns the status: 0 means the store landed.
; It may spuriously return 1 - that is legal - so the caller retries a
; bounded number of times before calling it a failure.
Procedure.i AtStxrMatched(*addr, value.i)
  ASM
    ldxr  x9, [x0]
    stxr  w10, x1, [x0]
    mov   x0, x10
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; Two store-exclusives from one load. The architecture clears the local
; monitor inside AArch64_ExclusiveMonitorsPass, before it has even
; looked at the result (arm-a64-instruction-set.txt:481582), so the
; SECOND one must fail. Returns first_status * 2 + second_status, so one
; number carries both: 1 means "first passed, second failed", which is
; the required answer.
Procedure.i AtStxrTwice(*addr)
  ASM
    movz  x9, #$1234
    ldxr  x11, [x0]
    stxr  w10, x9, [x0]
    mov   x11, x10
    stxr  w10, x9, [x0]
    lsl   x11, x11, #1
    add   x0, x11, x10
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; A plain store between the load and the store-exclusive. Returns the
; status. Expected 1 on every implementation anyone has met; PRINTED,
; not judged, because the architecture leaves room.
Procedure.i AtStxrAfterPlainStore(*addr)
  ASM
    ldxr  x9, [x0]
    movz  x11, #$7777
    str   x11, [x0]
    stxr  w10, x9, [x0]
    mov   x0, x10
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; Load exclusive from one address, store exclusive to another. IMPDEF -
; printed, not judged. See the header.
Procedure.i AtStxrCrossAddress(*from, *to)
  ASM
    ldxr  x9, [x0]
    movz  x11, #$3333
    stxr  w10, x11, [x1]
    mov   x0, x10
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ldar / stlr, which take no reservation at all. A round trip through
; memory: if this returns what it stored, the ordered accesses move the
; right bytes.
Procedure.i AtOrderedRoundTrip(*addr, value.i)
  ASM
    stlr  x1, [x0]
    ldar  x0, [x0]
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; A store-exclusive after an ldar. ldar is NOT an exclusive - o2 = 1 in
; the encoding - so it takes no reservation and this must fail. An
; implementation that got o2 backwards would pass every other check on
; this page.
Procedure.i AtStxrAfterLdar(*addr)
  ASM
    clrex
    ldar  x9, [x0]
    movz  x11, #$2222
    stxr  w10, x11, [x0]
    mov   x0, x10
  EndASM
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Printing. Copied from pi4FpState.pi4 rather than shared, for the
;  reason that file gives: a diagnostic that depends on another
;  diagnostic stops building when somebody tidies the other one.
;
;  No character literals - house rule. 48 is the code for the digit
;  zero, 55 is the letter A minus ten, 32 is a space.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = 15 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure PutDec(v.i)
  Define digits.i
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Dim buf.i(24)
  n = v
  If n < 0
    UartWrite(45)                      ; minus
    n = -n
  EndIf
  If n = 0
    UartWrite(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  digits = 0
  While n > 0
    buf(digits) = n % 10
    n = n / 10
    digits = digits + 1
  Wend
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    UartWrite(48 + buf(i))
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Row(*label, v.i)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWrite(36)                        ; the dollar sign that marks hex
  PutHex16(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure Note(*label, *text)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWriteStr(*text)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

; A judged check. Prints PASS or FAIL, the label, and what was seen.
Procedure Judge(*label, got.i, want.i)
  If got = want
    at_pass = at_pass + 1
    UartWriteStr("  PASS  ")
  Else
    at_fail = at_fail + 1
    UartWriteStr("  FAIL  ")
  EndIf
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWriteStr("  got ")
  PutDec(got)
  UartWriteStr(" want ")
  PutDec(want)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

; An unjudged observation: something the architecture leaves to the
; implementation. Reported so the next person knows what this silicon
; does, never asserted.
Procedure Observe(*label, got.i)
  UartWriteStr("  ----  ")
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWriteStr("  = ")
  PutDec(got)
  UartWriteStr("   (implementation defined - reported, not required)")
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define isar.i
  Define atomicfield.i
  Define midr.i
  Define i.i
  Define r.i
  Define status.i
  Define tries.i
  Define retries.i

  UartInit(115200)
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("pi4Atomics - ARMv8.0 exclusives on the silicon, and the LSE question")
  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteNl()

  midr = AtMidr()
  isar = AtIdIsar0()
  Row("MIDR_EL1        ", midr)
  Row("CurrentEL raw   ", AtCurrentEl())
  Row("ID_AA64ISAR0_EL1", isar)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  QUESTION ONE. ATOMIC is bits 23:20.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  atomicfield = (isar >> 20) & $F
  UartWriteStr("ID_AA64ISAR0_EL1.ATOMIC (bits 23:20) = ")
  PutDec(atomicfield)
  UartWriteNl()
  If atomicfield = 0
    Note("  verdict  ", "0b0000 = NI. FEAT_LSE is NOT implemented on this part.")
    Note("           ", "cas / swp / ldadd would be UNDEFINED here. The assembler")
    Note("           ", "is right to refuse them. Build atomics out of ldxr/stxr.")
  Else
    Note("  verdict  ", "NON-ZERO. This part DOES implement the LSE atomics, and")
    Note("           ", "A64Assembler.pbi's blanket refusal is now WRONG for it.")
    Note("           ", "Do not just delete the refusal - encode the family, cite")
    Note("           ", "it, and gate it. See A64IsLseAtomic in A64Assembler.pbi.")
  EndIf
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  QUESTION TWO, and the monitor's behaviour on this silicon.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  UartWriteStr("The exclusive monitor, on this board, with memory as it is now:")
  UartWriteNl()

  ; Check 1. A store-exclusive with the monitor provably open must fail.
  ; This is the deterministic one and it needs no retry.
  at_scratch[0] = $1111
  status = AtStxrWithNoReservation(@at_scratch[0])
  Judge("stxr after clrex fails            ", status, 1)
  Judge("...and did not write memory       ", at_scratch[0], $1111)

  ; Check 2. A matched pair. A spurious failure is legal, so this is
  ; retried a bounded number of times and the retries are reported.
  ; If this needs more than a handful of tries on an idle single core,
  ; something is interesting.
  at_scratch[0] = 0
  retries = 0
  status = 1
  tries = 0
  While status <> 0 And tries < 64
    status = AtStxrMatched(@at_scratch[0], $ABCD)
    tries = tries + 1
    If status <> 0
      retries = retries + 1
    EndIf
  Wend
  Judge("ldxr/stxr pair succeeds           ", status, 0)
  Judge("...and wrote the value            ", at_scratch[0], $ABCD)
  UartWriteStr("        spurious failures before it landed: ")
  PutDec(retries)
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  THIS IS THE CHECK THAT ANSWERS QUESTION TWO. If the pair above
  ;  never landed in 64 tries, exclusives do not work on this memory as
  ;  it is currently mapped, and every lock on this board needs the MMU
  ;  on with DRAM Normal. Say so in as many words rather than leaving it
  ;  to be inferred from a FAIL line.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  If status <> 0
    UartWriteNl()
    Note("STOP     ", "EVERY STORE-EXCLUSIVE REPORTED FAILURE, 64 times, on an idle")
    Note("         ", "single core with nothing contending. That is not spurious")
    Note("         ", "failure - the exclusive monitor does not report correctly")
    Note("         ", "on this memory as currently mapped.")
    Note("         ", "NOW LOOK AT THE LINE ABOVE THAT SAYS WHETHER THE VALUE WAS")
    Note("         ", "WRITTEN. If it passed, the failing stores ARE LANDING, and")
    Note("         ", "that is far worse than not working: every retry loop in")
    Note("         ", "Lib/atomic.pi4 performs its operation 1024 times and THEN")
    Note("         ", "returns #ATOMIC_GAVE_UP. An AtomicAdd of 1 moves the")
    Note("         ", "counter by 1024. Look for a counter 1024x too large.")
    Note("         ", "WHY: with the MMU off every access is Device, and the")
    Note("         ", "architecture does not guarantee exclusives outside Normal")
    Note("         ", "memory. The fix is Lib/mmu.pi4 with DRAM mapped Normal")
    Note("         ", "Inner-Shareable, and then re-running this program.")
    Note("         ", "UNTIL THEN: AtomicSelfTest() refuses, and nothing else in")
    Note("         ", "Lib/atomic.pi4 may be used. The encodings are not in")
    Note("         ", "question - a64_atomic_check.py checks 1078 of them against")
    Note("         ", "Arm's tables and an independent assembler. This is a")
    Note("         ", "memory-regime problem, not an assembler problem.")
    UartWriteNl()
  EndIf

  ; Check 3. Two store-exclusives from one load: the second must fail.
  at_scratch[0] = 0
  r = AtStxrTwice(@at_scratch[0])
  ; first*2 + second. 1 = first passed, second failed.
  Judge("second stxr of a pair fails       ", r, 1)

  ; Check 4. ldar takes no reservation.
  at_scratch[0] = 0
  Judge("stxr after ldar fails             ", AtStxrAfterLdar(@at_scratch[0]), 1)

  ; Check 5. The ordered accesses move the right bytes, both widths of
  ; the same word.
  Judge("stlr/ldar round trip              ", AtOrderedRoundTrip(@at_scratch[0], $FEEDFACE), $FEEDFACE)

  ; The two implementation-defined observations. Printed, never judged.
  at_scratch[0] = 0
  ; One line each - this language has no line continuation inside a
  ; call, which is why these are long.
  Observe("stxr after a plain store to the same word (1 = failed)", AtStxrAfterPlainStore(@at_scratch[0]))
  at_scratch[0] = 0
  at_other[0] = 0
  Observe("stxr to a different word than the ldxr (1 = failed)  ", AtStxrCrossAddress(@at_scratch[0], @at_other[0]))

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("RaspberryPi4/Lib/atomic.pi4, on this board:")
  UartWriteNl()

  ; THE GUARD, FIRST. This is the one call a real program makes before
  ; it uses any lock, and it is checked here before anything that
  ; depends on it - so its answer can be compared against the raw
  ; monitor results above rather than taken on trust.
  ;
  ; It is deliberately given at_scratch and not at_lock: it writes and
  ; restores whatever word it is given, and pointing it at a lock word
  ; that a real program might be holding would be a bad habit to
  ; demonstrate even where it is harmless.
  at_scratch[0] = $C0DE
  r = AtomicSelfTest(@at_scratch[0])
  UartWriteStr("  ----  AtomicSelfTest = ")
  PutDec(r)
  If r = #ATOMIC_OK
    UartWriteStr("  (OK - the monitor works on this memory)")
  Else
    UartWriteStr("  (REFUSES - and every check below is therefore expected to fail)")
  EndIf
  UartWriteNl()
  Judge("...and left the word untouched    ", at_scratch[0], $C0DE)

  ; The library. #ATOMIC_OK is 0, #ATOMIC_BUSY is 1, #ATOMIC_GAVE_UP 2.
  at_lock[0] = 0
  Judge("AtomicTryLock on a free lock      ", AtomicTryLock(@at_lock[0]), #ATOMIC_OK)
  Judge("...the lock is now held           ", at_lock[0], 1)
  Judge("AtomicTryLock on a held lock      ", AtomicTryLock(@at_lock[0]), #ATOMIC_BUSY)
  Judge("...and did not disturb it         ", at_lock[0], 1)

  ; A bounded wait on a lock nobody will ever release. This is the
  ; refusal, on hardware, and it is the whole argument for bounding the
  ; loop: without the bound this line is where the board stops
  ; answering.
  Judge("AtomicLock on a held lock refuses ", AtomicLock(@at_lock[0], 200), #ATOMIC_GAVE_UP)
  Judge("...and still did not take it      ", at_lock[0], 1)
  Judge("AtomicLock with a zero budget     ", AtomicLock(@at_lock[0], 0), #ATOMIC_GAVE_UP)

  AtomicUnlock(@at_lock[0])
  Judge("AtomicUnlock releases             ", at_lock[0], 0)
  Judge("AtomicLock on a free lock         ", AtomicLock(@at_lock[0], 200), #ATOMIC_OK)
  AtomicUnlock(@at_lock[0])

  at_counter[0] = 40
  Judge("AtomicCas with a matching value   ", AtomicCas(@at_counter[0], 40, 41), #ATOMIC_OK)
  Judge("...wrote the replacement          ", at_counter[0], 41)
  Judge("AtomicCas with a differing value  ", AtomicCas(@at_counter[0], 40, 99), #ATOMIC_BUSY)
  Judge("...and left the word alone        ", at_counter[0], 41)

  at_counter[0] = 0
  at_result = 0
  Judge("AtomicAdd                         ", AtomicAdd(@at_counter[0], 7, @at_result), #ATOMIC_OK)
  Judge("...added                          ", at_counter[0], 7)
  Judge("...reported the new value         ", at_result, 7)
  Judge("AtomicAdd with a negative delta   ", AtomicAdd(@at_counter[0], -3, @at_result), #ATOMIC_OK)
  Judge("...subtracted                     ", at_counter[0], 4)

  ; A THOUSAND INCREMENTS. Single-core, so this proves arithmetic and
  ; not exclusion - but a monitor that spuriously failed often enough to
  ; exhaust the bound would show up here and nowhere else, and a
  ; primitive that lost an update under repetition would land short.
  at_counter[0] = 0
  r = #ATOMIC_OK
  For i = 1 To 1000
    If AtomicAdd(@at_counter[0], 1, @at_result) <> #ATOMIC_OK
      r = #ATOMIC_GAVE_UP
    EndIf
  Next
  Judge("1000 x AtomicAdd all succeeded    ", r, #ATOMIC_OK)
  Judge("...counter reached 1000           ", at_counter[0], 1000)

  UartWriteNl()
  UartWriteStr("passed ")
  PutDec(at_pass)
  UartWriteStr("   failed ")
  PutDec(at_fail)
  UartWriteNl()
  If at_fail = 0
    Note("VERDICT  ", "every judged check passed on this silicon.")
  ElseIf r <> #ATOMIC_OK
    ; `r` still holds the AtomicSelfTest result. Saying this out loud
    ; matters: a reader who sees twelve FAIL lines and does not connect
    ; them to the one line at the top will go hunting the library for a
    ; bug that is not in it.
    Note("VERDICT  ", "AtomicSelfTest REFUSED, so the exclusive monitor does not")
    Note("         ", "work on this memory - and EVERY library failure above is")
    Note("         ", "explained by that one fact. Do not go looking for a bug in")
    Note("         ", "Lib/atomic.pi4. Map DRAM as Normal Inner-Shareable with")
    Note("         ", "Lib/mmu.pi4 and run this again; that is the experiment.")
  Else
    Note("VERDICT  ", "SOMETHING FAILED while AtomicSelfTest said the monitor")
    Note("         ", "works. That combination is NOT explained by the memory")
    Note("         ", "regime and is a real defect. Read the FAIL lines.")
  EndIf
  Note("NOTE     ", "one core ran all of this. Nothing here contended anything,")
  Note("         ", "so nothing here proves a lock excludes another core.")
  UartWriteNl()

  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
